Raw dev notes -- Bot's Big Leap: From Basic Answers to Bulletproof Reliability

This past sprint was a whirlwind for the WhatsApp bot. The project is essentially a conversational AI that lets users ask questions and get answers, pulling from a knowledge base (think a personal NotebookLM, but for WhatsApp). The goal is to make information super accessible and natural to interact with, right where people already chat.

**What I've been up to:**

A huge chunk of work went into making the bot more robust and user-friendly. I spent a lot of time on the **user experience around waiting**. Initially, I had these "hold on" filler messages, but after some user feedback, I actually disabled them by default – turns out, people just wanted the answer, not the chatter! But before that, I tried to make them better: reframing them as "Did you know?" facts, adding an hourglass, and carefully tuning the timing (from 30s to 45s, then 55s, and logging their fire times to see the cadence). I also fixed a nasty bug where a filler would sometimes land *after* the actual answer, which was just embarrassing.

**Conversation flow got a big upgrade too.** The bot now supports WhatsApp reply-quotes, meaning you can quote a previous message and the bot understands that context. Reactions (like a thumbs-up emoji) are now logged as feedback, which is super useful. I also fixed a critical bug with the conversation TTL clock – it was using `time.monotonic`, which resets on machine reboot, meaning conversations would just vanish. Switched to `time.time` for persistence.

**Analytics and engagement** were another focus. I added a content-blind analytics layer to track delivery failures and general usage, even built a local dashboard for it. To re-engage dormant users, I implemented a personalized nudge, crafting a "come back" message based on their last few interactions, but only within the WhatsApp 24-hour free-form window. And for sharing, I added a `/share` command that sends a contact card, making it easy for users to refer the bot to friends.

**Answer quality and presentation** saw improvements. Answers can now be split into parts with `[N/M]` indicators, and crucially, I added a "Sources" block for each `[n]` citation, showing where the information came from. This is huge for trust and transparency. I also started logging the raw answers and full source references for auditing.

Perhaps the biggest chunk of work was on **resilience and reliability**. I implemented a persistent inbound queue so messages aren't lost if the bot crashes mid-answer. Backend resilience got a major overhaul with increased timeouts, classified retries, a circuit breaker, and better error logging. There's now a dead-letter store for messages that truly can't be processed, a graceful shutdown mechanism, and an inbound-liveness watchdog. I even added a reconciliation job to retroactively answer any inbound questions that somehow slipped through the cracks. Plus, I tightened up the `/chat` DOM-drift canary to catch issues faster.

Finally, **caching** got some love. Answers are now cached, never expiring by default, and seeded from past Q&A. The bot also checks the cache for standalone questions, even outside an active conversation.

**What was hard or surprising:**

The `time.monotonic` bug was a classic "duh" moment – something so fundamental, but easy to overlook until a reboot hits you. The filler timing and cancellation logic was surprisingly fiddly; getting messages to land exactly when and where you want them in a chat app is always a dance. And the user feedback to disable fillers was a good, humbling reminder that sometimes what *I* think is a good idea isn't what users actually want. The sheer amount of work needed to make the bot truly resilient – persistent queues, dead letters, reconciliation – really hammered home how complex real-world systems are compared to a simple "hello world." It's not just about the happy path.

**Personal reflection:**

This period felt like a big step up in maturity for the project, and for me as a developer. I moved from just getting features out the door to really thinking about the user experience, the reliability, and the long-term maintainability. It was challenging, especially wrestling with all the edge cases for resilience, but also incredibly satisfying to see the system become so much more robust. It's a good reminder that building software isn't just about writing code, it's about anticipating failure, listening to users, and constantly refining.

**What's next:**

Group chat support (when Meta enables it), more user stats, and refining the nudge isolation.
